{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Na.</b> Some say: when it is now, that the flesh is raw, and in this way we have not found in all of Scripture. Others say it is from the root of כי הניא אביה אותה (Numbers 30:6), and the meaning is 'broken,' and this is not correct, for afterward it says ועצם לא תשברו בו (Exodus 12:46). The correct view in my eyes is that it has no parallel in Scripture, and its meaning is the opposite of 'cooked' — and that which is called חי, as in לא יוקח ממך בשר מבושל כי אם חי (1 Samuel 2:15). I have already said that most of the Arabic language resembles the Hebrew language, and raw flesh they call in Arabic ניי, and the letters alef, heh, vav, yod interchange for them in their language as in ours." "<b>Uvashel.</b> A noun of attribute, and its meaning is that it is cooked in water, for roasted by fire is also called 'cooked,' as it is written ובשלת ואכלת (Deuteronomy 16:7), ויבשלו את הפסח באש כמשפט (2 Chronicles 35:13). It is also possible that uvashel [means] 'cooked,' like royal food, by way of the double-boiler — for they place a broad copper vessel on the fire and fill half of it with water, and when they boil the water they place the meat pot with cold water on the boiling water, and thus the meat cooks, and this food is more beneficial than any food that is close to the fire. And I, the writer, am adding to the words of this sage as I heard from his mouth: uvashel is a noun of attribute on the pattern of כבד, זקן, and thus this uvashel, for the bet has a kamatz and the shin is raphe, and it is not a verb — on the pattern of כי כבד אכבדך (Numbers 22:17), הכה תכה (Deuteronomy 13:16). For a verb will never appear compounded except with past or future tense, like דבר ידבר (Exodus 4:14), הקדש הקדשתי (Judges 17:3); but a noun compounded with verb and participle does not occur. And so this בשל is like כבד, and for the feminine הזרוע בשלה (Numbers 6:19). And do not marvel what need there is to say two things, 'cooked' and 'boiled' — for there are two cookings, as the first explained: to place a vessel with the meat inside it within a vessel of boiling water; and for the plural, בשלים מבושלים, and they are 'wise ones, the wisest' (Proverbs 30:24), and thus for the singular one says חכם מחוכם, ברוך ומבורך. And there is a distinction in linguistic analysis between חכם and מחוכם, and thus between לבשל and מבושל, as he explained." "<b>Its head on its legs and on its innards.</b> Like עם, and likewise ויבאו האנשים על הנשים (Exodus 35:22). And the head and the legs shall be washed in water, as is the rule."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {1 "The text reads על דרך המשקיות; I translate 'double-boiler' literally from the mechanism described immediately after. The phrase is Ibn Ezra's descriptive label for the bain-marie technique."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:33:41.762141641Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}